abstract: >-
  The Regime-Consistent Measurement Principle (RCMP) is a methodological
  framework designed to prevent interpretive errors when measurements span
  multiple physical regimes. The principle states that an observable must be
  interpreted through the variable most directly coupled to the physical driver
  in the phenomenon's operative regime, with cross-regime mappings requiring
  explicit transformation and uncertainty propagation. The framework formalizes
  five core principles: driver proximity, regime tagging, proxy accounting,
  coordinate humility, and cross-validation. Application to galaxy rotation
  curve analysis demonstrates the framework's utility in distinguishing
  intra-galaxy variability from inter-galaxy physics.
